22:28:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Stop lights almost never use actual weight sensors. The vast majority of vehicle detection at intersections relies on **inductive-loop detectors**—and the common belief that you need a heavy vehicle to “trigger” the sensor is a myth. Here’s exactly how the real technology works, why people think it’s about weight, and what happens if a real weighing system is ever involved. --- ### 1. The real sensor: inductive-loop detectors These are loops of wire embedded in a saw-cut groove just below the pavement surface, usually in a rectangular shape (like 6×6 feet or a diamond shape). They operate on the principle of **electromagnetic induction**. **Physical setup** - Several turns of insulated wire are laid in the slot and sealed. - The loop connects to a roadside pull-box and then to a detector card in the traffic signal controller cabinet. **Electrical principle** - The detector card drives an alternating current (typically between 10 kHz and 200 kHz) through the loop, turning it into an inductor. - This forms part of an oscillator circuit whose resonant frequency is determined by the inductance of the loop and a capacitor in the detector. - When a vehicle enters the loop’s magnetic field, the metal parts (especially iron and steel) change the loop’s inductance. - The change in inductance shifts the oscillator frequency (or energy absorption in some designs), which the detector measures. **How it decides a vehicle is present** - The detector continuously monitors the inductance. A baseline “empty” inductance is stored. - When a vehicle moves over or rests on the loop, the inductance drops (or rises for some metals) by a detectable amount. The detector registers a **call**—a signal that a vehicle is waiting. - Sensitivity is adjustable: a threshold is set as a percentage of the empty inductance change. Even a bicycle with enough metal can trigger a properly tuned loop, but a full-carbon-fiber bike might not. **Why people confuse this with weight** - The sensor responds to **metal mass** (and its magnetic permeability), not weight. A large, heavy metal car changes inductance more than a small motorcycle—but a 200 lb block of aluminum might do little, while a 50 lb steel plate could trigger it. - Motorcycles often struggle not because they’re light, but because they have less conductive material in the right location. Some loops are intentionally skewed or cut with “quadrupole” loop geometry to improve detection of small vehicles. - The myth persists because heavier vehicles also tend to be bigger and metal-rich, so drivers wrongly attribute detection to weight. --- ### 2. Other common sensors at traffic lights Inductive loops are still widespread, but many cities now supplement or replace them with non-invasive sensors: - **Video cameras** (mounted on mast arms): analyze pixels for vehicles in defined zones. They can be fooled by shadows or snow. - **Radar (microwave)**: emits radio waves and detects Doppler shift or reflection from moving/ stationary vehicles. Excellent in bad weather. - **Magnetometers (wireless in-pavement)** or fluxgate sensors: detect the Earth’s magnetic field distortion caused by a vehicle’s metal. These are small, battery-powered pucks installed under the surface. - **Infrared sensors**: active or passive, measure heat or reflectivity. All of these feed presence or passage data to the traffic signal controller, but none measure weight. --- ### 3. Where weight sensors are used (not at stop lights) Actual weight sensors exist on roads, but they are **weigh-in-motion (WIM)** systems used for traffic data collection, enforcement of truck weight limits, or automatic tolling. These include: - **Piezoelectric sensors**: strips embedded in the road that generate a voltage when compressed; the voltage amplitude correlates with axle load. - **Bending plate or load cell scales**: steel plates with strain gauges that measure deflection as a wheel passes. - **Capacitive mats**, hydraulic cells, fiber-optic sensors. Those are far more expensive and bulky than needed for just detecting a waiting car. You’ll find them on highways leading to weigh stations, not at your local intersection. --- ### 4. How the signal controller uses the detection input Once a detection zone registers a call, the controller’s logic picks it up: - **Actuated signals**: The most common type. A detector sees a vehicle in a side street or left-turn lane, which places a “demand” for the green. The controller then serves that phase after completing any minimum green time on the main road and gaps in traffic (gap timing). - **Presence vs. pulse mode**: Loops can be set to provide a continuous “vehicle still there” signal (presence) or a short pulse when a vehicle enters. Presence mode holds the green longer if cars keep arriving. - **Locking memory**: Even if a vehicle moves forward out of the loop zone, the call can be “locked” until the green is served—this prevents missing a left-turner who edges past the sensor. The “weight” misconception often flares up when a car stops too far forward or too far back, completely avoiding the inductive loop’s magnetic field. The driver assumes their vehicle is too light, but it’s simply not over the wire. --- ### In summary Traffic lights **do not** use weight sensors. They use inductive loops (or cameras/radar) that detect changes in an electromagnetic field caused by a vehicle’s metal. The shift is interpreted as a vehicle call, and the signal controller changes the light accordingly. The only weight-measuring devices on roads are dedicated scales for weighing trucks at highway speeds—never for routine intersection control.
